WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Patchsentry helps teams inspect software changes using configured tests
and traceable evidence. This guide is public and the source is MIT
licensed.

2
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:28.000
Start with Node.js 24 and the no-key engine demo. Install public
dependencies once. The demonstration needs no account, API key, Docker
or live integration.

3
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:44.000
These are captured real Node assertions. The healthy fixture passes four
tests. A seeded strict comparison charges five dollars at exactly one
hundred dollars, where the expected fee is zero.

4
00:00:44.000 --> 00:01:00.000
The dashboard you see now is an illustrative sample workspace. Its
scores and findings show the interface; they are not executed customer
assessments.

5
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:16.000
This sample report shows how failures and suggested fixes are presented.
Required-check scores are not code coverage. AI findings remain
suggestions that need review.

6
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Inspect the actual verification records and limitations. The initial
own-release challenge detected two of four seeded faults. Regressions
later caught both misses; that is not a new held-out accuracy result.

7
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:48.000
The website coordinates work and keeps the hosted assessment Ledger. The
engine executes configured checks. The Java archive is optional and not
deployed by this project. Product source stays read-only.

8
00:01:48.000 --> 00:02:12.520
Contribute documentation, reproducibility checks, accessibility feedback
or a focused test. Existing external work is credited. Community
participation and staffing savings are never manufactured.
